# Owner-Facing Brevity (Mandatory)

The project owner is **not** a coder. Default replies must be **readable in plain language**, not padded with code they will skip.

## Default (talking to the owner)

- **Lead with the answer** — outcome, verdict, or decision in the first sentence or two.
- **Prose over paste** — explain what changed or how something works in short paragraphs or a tight table. Do **not** paste multi-line code blocks “for completeness.”
- **No factory dumps** — do not quote `bruiser_factory.gd`, harness functions, or long `ability_system` paths unless the owner **asked to see code** or a **single line** is the whole answer.
- **Spot checks / audits** — Bible text + behavior summary + match yes/no/noise. Skip implementation walkthroughs unless asked.
- **One reference max** when helpful — file path or symbol name inline (e.g. `bruiser_charge_strike` in `bruiser_factory.gd`), not a fenced block.
- **Changelogs** — still follow `living-sandbox-architect.mdc` Before/After, but keep **owner-visible prose** short; detail belongs in changelog sections, not repeated as code in the opening reply.

## When code in the reply is OK

- Owner explicitly asks: “show me the code,” “paste the diff,” “what does the function look like.”
- A **≤3-line** snippet is the only way to disambiguate (rare).
- Required **permission-gate** or **⚠ Global system exception** blocks (unchanged).

## Forbidden in owner-facing text

- Long fenced code blocks copied from the repo “so you can see it.”
- Stack-walking multiple files with line-number citations when a summary suffices.
- Repeating the same code in the reply **and** changelog **and** plan.

## Does not relax

- `global-systems-first.mdc`, `interpret-plan-permission.mdc`, `qa-after-gameplay-changes.mdc`, gauntlet banners, or auto-commit — those stay mandatory; just **don’t bury the headline under code**.
